# Schedule — Planning
#
# PMBOK Guide 6th ed., 6.2 Define Activities through 6.5 Develop Schedule.
#
# The timeline view draws the dates you enter. It does not calculate them:
# scheduling engine math (critical path, resource leveling, float) is out of
# scope for this product by the charter.

screen: schedule
title: Schedule
phase: Planning
process: 6.5 Develop Schedule
order: 50
requires: scope
teaches: |
  How activities, dependencies and dates fit together, and why a baseline is
  the thing that makes a schedule mean anything. By the end you should be able
  to explain what a critical path is and why a schedule that is always being
  adjusted is never late.
before: |
  The WBS. Activities come from work packages, so without one you are
  scheduling a list someone made up rather than the actual work.
after: |
  Progress reporting. Once there is a baseline, "on schedule" becomes a
  statement that can be checked rather than a feeling.
intro: |
  The schedule says when the work happens and in what order. Activities come
  from the work packages in the WBS, get sequenced by their dependencies, and
  are given durations and dates.

  This screen records the schedule and draws it. It deliberately does not
  compute it: the charter puts scheduling-engine math out of scope.
source: |
  PMBOK Guide, 6th ed., Project Schedule Management: 6.2 Define Activities,
  6.3 Sequence Activities, 6.4 Estimate Activity Durations and 6.5 Develop
  Schedule, all in the Planning process group.

field activities
  prompt: What gets done, in what order, and when?
  check rows>=3: A schedule needs more than a couple of rows before sequencing means anything.
  check column:start: An activity with no start date cannot be sequenced or tracked.
  check column:finish: An activity with no finish date has no duration, so nothing can be late.
  label: Schedule
  type: table
  origin: standard
  view: timeline
  title_column: activity
  start_column: start
  finish_column: finish
  progress_column: percent
  tone_column: status
  source: |
    6.5 Develop Schedule, output: the project schedule. Activities come from
    6.2 Define Activities, their order from 6.3 Sequence Activities, and their
    durations from 6.4 Estimate Activity Durations.
  why: |
    Each row is one activity with a start, a finish and what it waits on. The
    "depends on" column is the dependency: finish-to-start is the common one,
    meaning this cannot start until that one finishes.

    A dependency you did not write down is the one that surprises you. The
    longest chain of dependent activities is the critical path, and it is what
    decides the project's end date, whether or not anything calculates it for
    you.
  if_blank: |
    Work is done in whatever order it occurs to someone, and the finish date is
    a guess that nobody can defend or revise.
  example: |
    A4 | Build the board screen | 2026-09-21 | 2026-09-24 | A3 | 0 | Not started
  column ref
    label: ID
    type: line
    width: 1
  column activity
    label: Activity
    type: line
    width: 5
  column start
    label: Start
    type: date
    width: 2
  column finish
    label: Finish
    type: date
    width: 2
  column depends
    label: Depends on
    type: line
    width: 2
  column owner
    label: Owner
    type: line
    width: 2
  column percent
    label: % done
    type: number
    width: 1
  column status
    label: Status
    type: choice
    options: Not started=faint, In progress=accent, Done=ok, Blocked=danger, Late=danger
    width: 2

field basis
  prompt: How did you arrive at these durations?
  check filled: An estimate without its basis cannot be defended when it slips, or improved next time.
  label: Basis of estimates
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    6.4 Estimate Activity Durations, output: basis of estimates — the
    assumptions, the method used, and the confidence level.
  why: |
    It records how the durations were arrived at, so a later slip can be
    diagnosed instead of argued about. An estimate without its basis cannot be
    defended or improved.
  if_blank: |
    Every estimate looks equally solid, including the ones that were guesses.
  example: |
    Durations are single-point estimates from the delivery team, assuming
    uninterrupted days. No contingency reserve added.

field baseline
  prompt: What is the approved version of this schedule, and has it been rebaselined?
  check filled: Without a baseline there is nothing to be late against, so 'on schedule' means nothing.
  label: Schedule baseline note
  type: text
  origin: standard
  source: |
    6.5 Develop Schedule, output: the schedule baseline — the approved version
    of the schedule, changed only through formal change control.
  why: |
    The baseline is the version you measure against. Once it is approved,
    moving a date is a change request, not an edit. That is the whole point:
    without a baseline, a schedule that is always being adjusted is never late.
  if_blank: |
    There is nothing to be late against, so "on schedule" means nothing.
  example: |
    Baselined 2026-09-15 from the charter milestone dates. One rebaseline so
    far, logged on the Change Log screen.
